{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>My spirit shall not judge with man.</b> He would not take offense and My spirit would not contend with me on account of man." "<b>For ever.</b> For length of days. Here, 'My spirit judges within Me whether to destroy or to show mercy' — this judging shall not be in My spirit forever, that is, for length of days." "<b>In that he is also flesh.</b> Like בְּשֶׁגַם, that is, בִּשְׁבִיל שֶׁגַם — 'because this too is in him,' that he is flesh and even so he does not humble himself before Me. And what if he were fire or something hard? Like its parallel: ״עַד שֶׁקַּמְתִּי דְּבוֹרָה״ (Judges 5) — 'until that I, Deborah, arose'; and so ״שָׁאַתָּה מְדַבֵּר עִמִּי״ (ibid. 6) — 'that You speak with me'; so too בְּשַׁגַם — like בְּשֶׁגַם." "<b>And his days shall be, etc.</b> I will lengthen My anger against them up to 120 years, and if they do not return I will bring the Flood upon them. And if you say: from the birth of Japheth until the Flood is no more than 100 years — there is no early and late in the Torah (Pesachim 6): the decree had already been decreed twenty years before Noah begot children, and so we find in Seder Olam. There are many aggadic midrashim on לֹא יָדוֹן, but this is the clear plain sense of it."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:57:44.818287758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}